{"type": "post", "post": {"team": "quality-engineering", "channel": "cold-weather-testing", "user": "rachel.winters", "message": "Hey team, quick heads up - I've been reviewing the Annual Testing Report for 2024 and wanted to make sure everyone is aligned on our cold-weather testing protocols for Q2.", "create_at": 1740825600000}}
{"type": "post", "post": {"team": "quality-engineering", "channel": "cold-weather-testing", "user": "marcus.chen", "message": "Good timing Rachel. I was actually about to ask about the temperature thresholds. Are we sticking with the same ranges from last year?", "create_at": 1740825900000}}
{"type": "post", "post": {"team": "quality-engineering", "channel": "cold-weather-testing", "user": "rachel.winters", "message": "Yes, the 2024 report confirms it. Cold-weather testing should be conducted at **-25\u00b0C, -15\u00b0C, and -5\u00b0C** to ensure better endurance coverage. These three temperature points give us the best data spread.", "create_at": 1740826200000}}
{"type": "post", "post": {"team": "quality-engineering", "channel": "cold-weather-testing", "user": "david.martinez", "message": "Makes sense. The -25\u00b0C test really pushed our battery packs to the limit during the Fairbanks trials. Glad we're keeping that in the protocol.", "create_at": 1740826500000}}
